        body: `The Sharpe ratio measures risk-adjusted performance: portfolio return minus the risk-free rate, divided by standard deviation of portfolio returns. A Sharpe ratio of 0.6 means you earn 0.6 units of excess return per unit of risk taken. The S&P 500 has historically achieved a Sharpe of approximately 0.5 to 0.6. Higher is better; comparing Sharpe ratios across strategies is more informative than comparing raw returns.

CAPM (Capital Asset Pricing Model) extended this framework by relating each security's expected return to its beta, or market sensitivity. Expected return equals the risk-free rate plus beta times the market risk premium. Alpha is the return above what CAPM predicts: consistently positive alpha represents genuine skill or structural advantage; consistently negative alpha represents underperformance adjusted for risk taken.`,
