        body: `A capital gain occurs when you sell an asset for more than you paid. The holding period determines the tax rate. Short-term capital gains (assets held one year or less) are taxed as ordinary income at your marginal rate, up to 37%. Long-term capital gains (held more than one year) are taxed at preferential rates: 0% for lower incomes, 15% for most investors, and 20% for the highest earners.

This distinction is worth real money. A $10,000 gain taxed as ordinary income at a 24% marginal rate costs $2,400. The same gain taxed at the 15% long-term rate costs $1,500. Waiting just past the one-year mark saves $900 on this single transaction. Over a lifetime of investing, systematically qualifying for long-term rates adds up to tens of thousands of dollars.`,
