        body: `Congress created the Alternative Minimum Tax in 1969 after discovering that 155 high-income Americans had paid zero federal income tax by using legal deductions. The AMT is a parallel tax system with fewer deductions and different rates, designed to ensure high earners pay a minimum amount of tax regardless of how many deductions they claim.

For tax years after 2017, the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act substantially increased the AMT exemption amounts and phase-out thresholds, dramatically reducing the number of taxpayers subject to AMT. The AMT exemption in 2025 is $137,000 for married filing jointly ($88,100 single), phasing out at higher incomes. Historically, millions of middle-class taxpayers were caught by AMT due to non-inflation-indexed thresholds; this has been largely corrected.`,
