        heading: 'How Pass-Through Taxation Works',
        body: `Most small businesses in the United States are taxed as pass-through entities: sole proprietorships, partnerships, S corporations, and single-member LLCs all pass business income through to the owner's personal tax return. The business itself pays no federal income tax; the owner reports the income and pays tax at individual rates.

This differs from C corporations, which pay corporate tax (21% flat rate after 2017) and then shareholders pay a second tax on dividends or capital gains when they extract money. The pass-through structure avoids this double taxation and is generally more efficient for small businesses, though C corporations have advantages for businesses retaining significant earnings for reinvestment.`,
