        heading: 'Audit Types, Selection, and Your Rights',
        body: `Correspondence audits (conducted by mail) examine specific items on your return and are the most common type. Office audits require you to meet at an IRS office with documentation. Field audits occur at your home or business and are reserved for complex or high-dollar situations. Overall, individual audit rates are low: approximately 0.2% for incomes under $200,000, rising to about 2% for incomes over $1 million.

Audit selection is mostly algorithmic. The DIF (Discriminant Index Function) score compares your return to statistical norms for your income level and business type. Returns with unusually large deductions relative to income, significant business losses, high cash-intensive businesses, or large charitable deductions relative to income are more likely selected. Your rights during an audit include the right to representation, the right to appeal any decision, and the right to a Collection Due Process hearing before enforced collection.`,
