        body: `Cryptocurrency is treated as property by the IRS, not currency. Every transaction involving cryptocurrency potentially triggers a taxable event. Buying crypto and holding it is not taxable. Selling crypto for dollars, trading one cryptocurrency for another, and using crypto to purchase goods or services are all taxable events that require calculating gain or loss based on the difference between your cost basis and the fair market value at the time of the transaction.

This creates an enormous record-keeping burden. If you have traded actively across multiple exchanges over several years, you may have thousands of individual taxable transactions requiring accurate cost basis tracking. The first-in-first-out (FIFO) method is the default, but specific identification (selling the highest-basis coins first to minimize gains) is permissible with adequate documentation and can significantly reduce taxes.`,
