        body: `Effective tax planning is not about minimizing this year's taxes in isolation but minimizing taxes over your entire lifetime, including your estate's taxes. These perspectives sometimes conflict: aggressively reducing taxes this year through deductions and deferrals may create larger tax obligations in future years when circumstances change.

The single most powerful lifetime tax planning concept is tax bracket arbitrage: paying taxes during years when your marginal rate is lower and deferring or avoiding taxes in years when your rate is higher. This requires projecting your income across decades including retirement: when do you expect income to be low (early retirement before Social Security), and how can you accelerate income or recognition into those years?`,
