        heading: 'Key Strategies That Work',
        body: `Deduction bunching: consolidating itemizable deductions into alternating years to exceed the standard deduction threshold in high-deduction years while taking the standard deduction in low-deduction years. Charitable contributions, medical expenses (if significant), and prepaid state taxes all can be timed.

Income splitting: shifting income to family members in lower tax brackets through legitimate means, including hiring children in a family business at reasonable wages, setting up a family limited partnership with annual gifts to lower-bracket members, and structuring trusts that allocate income to lower-bracket beneficiaries. The kiddie tax limits income shifting to minor children. Roth conversions in low-income years lock in permanently tax-free growth by paying a lower rate now than would apply when traditional accounts would otherwise be withdrawn.`,
