        body: `A thin credit file, meaning little or no credit history, creates the credit paradox: you need credit to build credit. Several proven paths break this cycle without requiring existing credit. A secured credit card requires a cash deposit (typically $200 to $500) that becomes your credit limit. You use it for small routine purchases, pay the full balance monthly, and after 12 to 18 months of responsible use the issuer typically upgrades you to an unsecured card and returns the deposit. Capital One Secured and Discover It Secured are highly regarded options.

Becoming an authorized user on a parent or partner's credit card is the fastest path: their entire account history immediately appears on your credit report. If they have a 10-year-old card with perfect payment history, you instantly benefit from that history. This only helps if the primary cardholder has excellent credit habits.`,
