        body: `A mortgage is a secured loan where the property itself serves as collateral. You borrow a principal amount, agree to repay it with interest over a specified term (typically 15 or 30 years), and make fixed monthly payments. Each payment contains both interest and principal: early in the loan, most of each payment is interest; as the loan ages, proportionally more goes to principal reduction. This process is called amortization.

On a $400,000 mortgage at 7% for 30 years, your monthly payment is approximately $2,661. In the first payment, roughly $2,333 is interest and only $328 reduces principal. In year 15, the split is approximately $1,741 interest and $920 principal. By year 30, payments are almost entirely principal. This front-loaded interest structure means making extra principal payments early dramatically reduces total interest cost.`,
