        body: `Leasing provides lower monthly payments for a newer vehicle but builds no equity. At lease end, you return the car with no asset and must lease or buy another. Lease payments represent the vehicle's depreciation during your term plus financing charges plus fees. Leasing makes economic sense when you prioritize lower payments, always want a new vehicle, drive under mileage limits (typically 10,000 to 15,000 miles annually), and do not place high value on vehicle ownership.

Buying and driving a car until it has no value is almost always the most cost-effective long-term approach. The break-even against leasing typically occurs around year 4 to 5 of ownership, when the loan is paid off and the continuing cost drops to maintenance and insurance only. Total 10-year cost of ownership of a purchased vehicle typically runs 40% to 50% less than the equivalent cost of perpetually leasing.`,
