        heading: 'Chapter 7 vs Chapter 13',
        body: `Chapter 7 liquidation bankruptcy discharges most unsecured debts (credit cards, medical bills, personal loans) within three to four months. The trustee may liquidate non-exempt assets to pay creditors, but most filers have primarily exempt assets. Eligibility requires passing the means test: income below the state median for your household size, or discretionary income insufficient to repay a meaningful amount of debt.

Chapter 13 reorganization bankruptcy allows keeping all assets in exchange for committing to a three- to five-year repayment plan where you pay disposable income to a trustee who distributes to creditors. Chapter 13 allows catching up on mortgage arrears to save a home, paying non-dischargeable taxes over time, and keeping property that would be liquidated in Chapter 7. Chapter 13 requires regular income to fund the repayment plan.`,
