        body: `Capital refers to assets beyond income: savings, investments, retirement accounts, and other property. Capital provides a cushion if income is disrupted and demonstrates financial discipline and commitment. A borrower with six months of reserves (enough to make payments for six months without any income) presents substantially lower risk than one with no reserves.

Collateral is the asset securing the loan, which the lender can seize if the borrower defaults. For mortgages, the property is collateral; for auto loans, the vehicle. Collateral quality affects the loan-to-value ratio the lender offers. Character is the most subjective factor: credit history, payment patterns, length of relationships with financial institutions, and sometimes personal references. Character is largely captured by the credit score but also informed by the stability of the borrower's overall financial and residential history.`,
