        body: `Inflation measures the rate at which prices across the economy are rising. The Consumer Price Index (CPI) is the most widely cited measure: it tracks prices of a fixed basket of goods and services consumed by urban households. Core CPI excludes volatile food and energy prices to reveal underlying inflation trends. The Federal Reserve targets 2% annual inflation as measured by the PCE (Personal Consumption Expenditures) price index, which uses different weightings and methodology than CPI.

For individuals, experienced inflation often differs from official measures. Healthcare inflation has consistently exceeded overall CPI for decades. College tuition has risen at three to four times the general inflation rate since the 1980s. Housing costs (especially rent) have recently risen at multiples of measured CPI. Your personal inflation rate depends heavily on your specific consumption pattern.`,
