        body: `Neobanks (also called challenger banks or digital banks) are fintech companies that offer banking products without traditional brick-and-mortar branches. Most neobanks are not banks themselves: they partner with FDIC-insured banks to hold deposits, effectively acting as customer interface layers. Chime partners with Stride Bank and Bancorp Bank. SoFi received its own bank charter in 2022 and is now a licensed bank. Varo received its bank charter in 2020.

This partnership model allows neobanks to offer FDIC-insured accounts (confirmed by checking the partner bank's FDIC certificate, not just the neobank's claims) without the regulatory burden and capital requirements of a bank charter. The risk: when a neobank fails (several have), the FDIC-insured deposits at the partner bank are protected, but access may be disrupted during the transition.`,
