        body: `The 2008 financial crisis began in US residential mortgage markets and cascaded into a global financial system failure. The sequence: subprime mortgages issued with minimal underwriting were packaged into mortgage-backed securities (MBS) rated AAA by rating agencies, purchased by banks and investors globally. When housing prices declined nationally for the first time in decades, these securities lost value, triggering losses at financial institutions that had borrowed heavily to purchase them.

Lehman Brothers' bankruptcy on September 15, 2008 was the pivotal moment: it proved that a major investment bank could be allowed to fail, triggering a panic that froze credit markets globally. Money market funds broke the buck, commercial paper markets seized, and lending between banks essentially stopped. The Federal Reserve and Treasury responded with unprecedented interventions to prevent complete financial system collapse.`,
